<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lord Roberts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts, was a British field marshal. He won the Victoria Cross during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>, became famous for his march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a> in 1880 and was commander-in-chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1885 to 1893. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> he took command in 1900, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, and proclaimed the annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-roberts-1st-earl-roberts.jpg" alt="Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Library of Ireland on The Commons · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Lord_Roberts_AKA_%22Bobs%22_(25136980491).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick Sleigh Roberts was born at Cawnpore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on 30 September 1832, the son of General Sir Abraham Roberts, of an Anglo-Irish family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-waterford">County Waterford</a>, who then commanded the 1st Bengal European Regiment. His mother was Isabella Bunbury, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. He was educated at Eton, Sandhurst and the Addiscombe Military Seminary, and entered the East India Company Army as a second lieutenant in the Bengal Artillery on 12 December 1851. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> he fought at the siege and capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and the relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, and won the Victoria Cross for actions at Khudaganj on 2 January 1858.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Roberts transferred to the British Army in 1861 and served in the Umbeyla campaign of 1863, the Abyssinian expedition of 1867 and 1868 and the Lushai campaign of 1871 and 1872. In October 1878 he took command of the Kurram Valley Field Force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a>. His success at the Battle of Peiwar Kotal in December 1878 brought him the thanks of Parliament and promotion to major general. After his Indian command he became commander-in-chief of British forces in Ireland from 1 October 1895, was promoted field marshal in May 1895, and published a memoir, Forty-one Years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in 1897.</p><p>On 23 December 1899 he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a> as chief of staff to take overall command in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, replacing General Redvers Buller after a string of early defeats. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> on 10 January 1900. After the relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> he forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-cronje">Piet Cronjé</a> to surrender with about 4,000 men at Paardeberg on 27 February 1900 and captured the Free State capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> on 13 March. His attempt to reorganise the army's supply system on the Indian model contributed to a typhoid epidemic that cost the British more men than combat did. He became the last Commander-in-Chief of the Forces on 3 January 1901; the post was abolished in February 1904.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In retirement Roberts was president of the National Service League from 1905 to 1914 and campaigned for compulsory military training to prepare for a great European war; his speeches appeared in 1907 as A Nation in Arms. He died of pneumonia at St Omer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on 14 November 1914 while visiting Indian troops, lay in state in Westminster Hall and was buried in St Paul's Cathedral after a state funeral. His son Frederick was killed at the Battle of Colenso in December 1899 and was awarded a posthumous Victoria Cross. A statue of Roberts by Harry Bates was unveiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1898, with copies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and on Horse Guards Parade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After early Boer successes, the British counter-offensive under <b>Lord Roberts</b> relieved Kimberley and trapped Piet Cronjé’s army, which included many Free Staters, at Paardeberg, where about 4,000 men surrendered on 27 February 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Early Boer successes gave way to the British advance under <b>Lord Roberts</b>: Johannesburg fell on 31 May and Pretoria on 5 June 1900, and Britain proclaimed the annexation of the Transvaal on 1 September 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kanpur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Saint-Omer, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.47,80.33&amp;year=1885" aria-label="Kanpur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6669 1192 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7119 1462h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7119 1462h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.47,80.33&amp;year=1885">What was here in 1885? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
